{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Thus you shall say to my lord, to Esau: Thus says your servant Jacob</b> He instructed them that they should say, \"To my lord, to Esau, are we,\" meaning we are his men or emissaries to him, and that they should say to him, \"Thus says your servant Jacob: I sojourned with Laban.\" Its parallel is in the passage (Genesis 32:18), \"To whom do you belong?\" Or perhaps Jacob referred to him in their presence as \"my lord Esau,\" to caution them not to mention him even outside his presence except in a manner of honor, when they see that their master calls him \"my lord.\" Know that this honor which Jacob paid his brother out of his fear, saying \"my lord\" and \"your servant,\" was because the custom for the younger is to give precedence and honor to the firstborn as though he were his father, as the Torah itself hinted to us, regarding \"your elder brother\" (Leviticus 10:14, Deuteronomy 21:17). Now Jacob had taken both his birthright and his blessing, and Esau hated him for them; so now he would show him as though that recognition were nothing to him, and that he conducts himself toward him as toward a firstborn and a father, in order to remove the hatred from his heart."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:17.400767163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}